How stars and popularity work
These follow the Burger Bar rule: accuracy counts, speed never does.
• Stars come from how well orders match their tickets. A restaurant's star rating rises as the player finishes good shifts there. It never goes down.
• Popularity grows with each good shift and decides how busy the restaurant looks on the court. It doesn't fade while the player is away, so nothing is lost by playing one restaurant for a while or taking a break.
• Stars and popularity are the same track: as a restaurant's stars go up, so does its crowd on the court.
The shared engine
Every restaurant runs on the Burger Bar engine as its own pack. That means each one gets the assembly line, the five-choice limit, Add or Skip, the end-of-order review, Simple and Timed cooking with automatic actions, accuracy scoring, the tutorial, gross-outs with visual cues and a manager's warning, expression poses, voices and the script file.
Each restaurant changes only its ingredients, its "cooking" step and its cast.
