The script
The script is written in full now, so the game is funny from the first playable build. It's extensive, so lines rarely repeat, and it lives in each pack's editable script file, so lines can be rewritten at any time before the voices are recorded.
Every character gets lines for every moment they can be in, with several variations each:
• Customers: greetings, ordering (including their usual and their running gags), small talk while the food is made, reactions for three, two and one stars, naming exactly what was wrong, spotting a gross-out that got through, and saying goodbye. The regulars also get lines specific to each restaurant, and the occasional mention of another restaurant on the court.
• Mitch: shift openers, tutorial lines for every phase in every restaurant, warnings before mistakes in the tutorial, a gross-out line for every gross-out, Timed-mode heads-ups, one-liners after reactions, five-star celebrations, and jokes about his uniform in each restaurant.
• Unique customers: a full set for their own restaurant, built around their gag (Tony and pineapple, Inferno and hot sauce, Jitters and caffeine, and so on).
• The court: shoppers' chatter, storefront announcements, and the celebration when a restaurant earns its gold sign.
The Burger Bar's existing rules for when Mitch talks still apply everywhere: scan speech always wins, he speaks into silence, no line repeats in a shift, and nobody ever comments on the player's speed or skill.
Build order
1. Everything is built with the full script played through per-character text to speech. Recording the voices is the final pass, once the script is settled. The Burger Bar's remaining voice recording waits until then too.
2. Build the food court: the main menu, shared settings, the top-down U-shaped court, stars and crowds, unlocking, and the Burger Bar moved under it so it opens straight to the counter.
3. Build the six new restaurants as packs on the engine, with art for each (backdrops, props, food, unique customers and Mitch's uniform poses), starting with the Pizza Parlor since the engine already has a test pizza pack. The regulars need no new art.
4. Ben playtests the court and each restaurant, and the press counts and timing get tuned from that.
